<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!-- No engine-side ceiling: a query returns what it asks for.

     THIS FILE IS LOAD-BEARING, not a tuning knob. Every field below is one the
     engine refuses to take from a request (`maxHits`/`maxOffset` fail with
     "must be specified in a query profile"; so does `grouping.globalMaxGroups`),
     so a query profile is the ONLY place they can be set — a deployment that
     replaces this file must carry every field over.

     maxHits/maxOffset: Vespa's own defaults are 400 and 1000, and a query
     asking for more is REJECTED (`N hits requested, configured limit: 400`),
     not trimmed. Int.MAX_VALUE is how you say "don't cap"; the only bound left
     is the caller's own `limit` (and the host's memory).

     The sentinel itself is FREE — measured 2026-08-05, 3000-doc corpus, median
     of 25. Vespa sizes its hit collector from what actually MATCHES, not from
     what was asked for, so a selective query pays nothing for a huge ceiling
     (12 matches: 10.07ms at hits=50, 6.66ms at hits=2147483647) and a broad one
     pays for the docs it RETURNS, not the ceiling (3000 matches: 29.47ms at
     hits=3000, 24.74ms at hits=2147483647). Cost tracks the answer's size,
     which is what "return everything" means and cannot be traded away — so a
     limit-less REQ is not an exposure to be capped here. Accepting any limit,
     including none, is the store contract: IEventStore.query() returns a List,
     so materializing the match set is inherent, not a choice this layer makes.

     grouping.globalMaxGroups: while this ceiling is on, Vespa fails any
     grouping pipeline that has no max() outright ("Cannot return unbounded
     number of groups"). Every aggregation this store issues — count, distinct
     count, kind histogram, distinct authors — is deliberately max()-less so it
     answers over the whole match set, so the ceiling has to be off. -1 is the
     documented "disabled" value; see EventYql.grouping, which disables the two
     per-request ceilings (defaultMaxGroups/defaultMaxHits) that DO travel as
     query parameters.

     timeout + ranking.softtimeout.enable: Vespa gives a query 500 ms and then,
     because soft timeout is ON by default, STOPS MATCHING AND RETURNS WHAT IT
     HAS — HTTP 200, a short result, `coverage.full: false`. A caller asking for
     everything would silently get part of it. A filter with no `limit` is a
     request for the whole match set and is allowed to take as long as that
     takes, so the deadline goes to Vespa's maximum (`Query.setTimeout` rejects
     anything >= 1000000000 ms, so this is the largest legal value — do NOT
     "max" it to 2147483647, that fails every query) and soft timeout is turned
     off, making a deadline hit a loud error instead of a quiet half-answer.
     VespaEventIndex checks `coverage.full` on every response, so degradation
     from any other cause cannot pass as a complete answer either.

     Deciding a policy cap is the OPERATOR's call, not the library's: lower
     these if you want the engine to refuse expensive queries — but note that
     lowering globalMaxGroups breaks the aggregation paths above, and that
     lowering the timeout while soft timeout is off turns slow queries into
     errors. A full-corpus dump should use a document/v1 visit
     (VespaEventIndex.visitIds) rather than a huge search — it streams instead
     of materializing. -->
<query-profile id="default">
    <field name="maxHits">2147483647</field>
    <field name="maxOffset">2147483647</field>
    <field name="grouping.globalMaxGroups">-1</field>
    <field name="timeout">999999999ms</field>
    <field name="ranking.softtimeout.enable">false</field>
</query-profile>
